  "worked_solution": "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are  ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)=-\\infty $ and  ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)}{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x} & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)]}^{\u2032}}{{[\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x]}^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{-\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\cancel{-\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}}{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x\\cancel{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
